# Committed pronunciation supplement — the permanent human override layer.
#
# CMUdict text (`WORD  P1 P2 ...`, ARPAbet with stress digits 0/1/2), merged
# over the pinned upstream inputs by `npm run build:index` (ADR-0009). Unlike
# the rest of data/, this file is committed and survives the rebuild.
#
#   - A word absent upstream is ADDED and granted wordhood (but a name is never
#     laundered into a word). It carries no prevalence, so it tiers as a Bonus
#     Word until the deferred prevalence machinery lands (#56).
#   - A word already upstream has its pronunciation CORRECTED — the entry here
#     OVERRIDES the upstream reading, it does not add a variant alongside it.
#
# A correction is a rhyme-verdict change, so keep it deliberate and note why.
#
# THIS FILE IS WHERE A WRONG PRONUNCIATION GETS FIXED — not a new rule.
# Reading manufacture (normalisation, coverage derivation) is frozen as of
# 2026-07-30: seven normalisation rules, thirty affix rules, closed sets. See
# ADR-0011. A verdict that comes out wrong is corrected here, or in
# data/names.txt (a name that reached the word list) or data/words.txt (junk
# with wordhood). All three are global — a fix applies to every Puzzle the word
# appears in, which is what makes the nightly workload decay instead of recur.
# Per-puzzle answer-set overlays are barred for the same reason.

# --- Adds (word valid, but absent from CMUdict so it had no reading) ---

# overjoy (#52): derived from the upstream inflection `overjoyed OW2 V ER0 JH
# OY1 D` by dropping the final D — rhymes on OY, with `joy`.
overjoy OW2 V ER0 JH OY1

# airburst (#48): air (EH1 R) + burst (B ER1 S T), the compound taking secondary
# stress on the final syllable so it rhymes on ER S T, with `burst`.
airburst EH1 R B ER2 S T

# --- Corrections (correct engine, wrong upstream stress) ---

# viceroy (#53): upstream `V AY1 S R OY0` leaves the final diphthong unstressed,
# so the rhyme wrongly anchored on AY. The final -roy carries real secondary
# stress; OY0 -> OY2 makes it rhyme on OY, with `oy`.
viceroy V AY1 S R OY2

# bratwurst (#46): upstream `B R AE1 T W ER0 S T` leaves -wurst unstressed, so
# the rhyme wrongly anchored on AE. The loanword's final syllable carries real
# secondary stress; ER0 -> ER2 makes it rhyme on ER S T, with `burst`.
bratwurst B R AE1 T W ER2 S T

# --- Corrections (wrong upstream phonemes) ---

# hurricane (#96): upstream carries a second reading with a spurious trailing Z
# — `HH AH1 R AH0 K EY2 N Z`, which looks like a `hurricanes` line that lost its
# s. It put `hurricane` in the plural key EY N Z, so it was accepted as a rhyme
# for `canes` and `planes`. Both readings are asserted here, Z removed: the
# `AH1 R` first syllable is a legitimate US variant and is kept, so this drops
# the Z without picking one of the two pronunciations. EY N, with `cane`.
hurricane HH ER1 AH0 K EY2 N
hurricane(1) HH AH1 R AH0 K EY2 N

# anglos, altos, bimbos, bios, burritos, campesinos, centavos, cheerios,
# cruzados, latinos, lobos, narcos, winos (#85): thirteen `-os` plurals CMUdict
# transcribes with a final S where the real sound is Z — an /oʊz/ plural, like
# `heroes`, not the genuine /oʊs/ singular `dose`/`sucrose` end in. Eleven reach
# the `dose` family (`OW S`) only through stress promotion, because their bad
# reading is unstressed (`OW0 S`); `altos` and `centavos` carry the bad `S`
# already stressed (`OW2 S`), so they reach `OW S` with no promotion at all.
# Every one is corrected to end Z. `anglos`'s upstream second reading duplicates
# its first once the S is fixed, so it collapses to one reading here; `altos`'s
# two readings stay genuinely distinct pronunciations, so both are asserted,
# the second corrected in place.
anglos AE1 NG G L OW0 Z
altos AE1 L T OW0 Z
altos(1) AO1 L T OW2 Z
bimbos B IH1 M B OW0 Z
bios B AY1 OW0 Z
burritos B ER0 IY1 T OW0 Z
campesinos K AE2 M P EH0 S IY1 N OW0 Z
centavos S EH0 N T AA1 V OW2 Z
cheerios CH IH1 R IY0 OW0 Z
cruzados K R UW2 Z AA1 D OW0 Z
latinos L AA0 T IY1 N OW0 Z
lobos L OW1 B OW0 Z
narcos N AA1 R K OW0 Z
winos W IY1 N OW0 Z

# --- Adds (family sweep: `-ule` words against target `UW L`, #71) ---
#
# The prevalence norms hold 60 words ending in `-ule`; CMUdict has readings for
# only 22 of them (`capsule`, `granule`, `module`, `molecule`, `ridicule`,
# `vestibule`, `mule`, `yule`, `joule`, `tubule`, `nodule`, `ovule`, `boule`,
# `saccule`, `minuscule`, `virgule`, and a handful more). The other 38 are
# invisible to the game: wordhood is already granted (`words.txt` says yes for
# every one, per `npm run editor:add -- --words ... --rhymeKey "UW L"`), so the
# only defect is the missing reading. None is a name.
#
# Every reading below either (a) is spliced onto a stem CMUdict already reads
# (`duct`, `barb`, `gem`, `plume`, `sphere`, `mount`, `gallus`, `hierarchy`,
# `amygdala`, `schedule`), (b) copies an existing supplement-family word's own
# stored digits verbatim (`molecule`, `saccule`, `minuscule`), or (c) is a
# plain hand-authored reading for a suffix this file already has direct
# precedent for (`globule` next to `capsule`/`module`; the loanwords `ampoule`,
# `cagoule`). Where the final `-ule` syllable's stress is not independently
# confirmed, it is left `UW0` rather than guessed `UW2` — the engine's own
# stress-promotion rule (ADR-0009, `normalise.ts`) reaches a full unstressed
# vowel in a closed final syllable regardless, so the rhyme verdict is
# identical either way and no digit is asserted that isn't earned.
#
# Three words on the original 38-word list are deliberately left out —
# `tule`, `gule`, `guayule` — because the judgement came out "genuinely
# unsure", not "yes": see the #71 report for the specific doubt on each.

# macromolecule: absent from CMUdict, but `molecule` is (`M AA1 L AH0 K Y
# UW2 L`) and its own final syllable's stress is reused unchanged. `macro-` is
# not one of this repo's configured prefixes (affixes.ts), so this is
# hand-authored rather than derived, by direct analogy to how `over-`/`under-`
# are composed there: an unstressed/secondary prefix, never primary.
macromolecule M AE2 K R OW0 M AA1 L AH0 K Y UW2 L

# globule: hand-authored. Real `/ˈɡlɑːbjuːl/`, the same shape as `capsule`/
# `module` (stressed first syllable, closed final syllable) but with the full
# vowel `capsule` lacks — `capsule`'s final schwa (`AH0 L`) is a genuinely
# different, unrelated sound, not evidence against this reading.
globule G L AA1 B Y UW0 L

# pustule: hand-authored. Real `/ˈpʌstʃuːl/` (also `/ˈpʌstjuːl/`); the
# palatalised `-chuul` variant is at least as common in US speech as `-tyool`.
pustule P AH1 S CH UW0 L

# reticule: hand-authored, same shape as `ridicule` (`R IH1 D AH0 K Y UW2 L`,
# already upstream) but the final digit is not copied — left `UW0`,
# stress-promotion reaches it the same way.
reticule R EH1 T AH0 K Y UW0 L

# glandule: hand-authored, real `/ˈɡlændʒuːl/`, the `d`+`y` palatalising to
# `jh` the way `graduate`/`individual` do. Same closed-final-syllable shape as
# `module`.
glandule G L AE1 N JH UW0 L

# ampoule: hand-authored. Common medical term, real `/ˈæmpuːl/`, `AM-pool`
# with clearly audible stress on both syllables (not a diminutive suffix
# reading at all, so given its own explicit secondary rather than left `UW0`).
ampoule AE1 M P UW2 L

# papule, stipule, cellule, pinnule, barbule, ductule, floccule, bascule,
# amygdule, plumule, noctule, monticule, fascicule, animalcule, frustule,
# pilule, gemmule, gallinule, hierodule, spherule, crepuscule: hand-authored,
# the same closed-final-syllable shape as `globule` above (stressed stem +
# `Y UW0 L`, promotable). `ductule` (duct `D AH1 K T`), `barbule` (barb
# `B AA1 R B`), `gemmule` (gem `JH EH1 M`), `plumule` (plume `P L UW1 M`),
# `spherule` (sphere `S F IH1 R`), `noctule` (nocturnal's `N AA0 K T` root),
# `amygdule` (amygdala's `AE2 M IH0 G D` root, independently stressed here
# since `amygdule` is a shorter word than `amygdala`), and `hierodule`
# (hierarchy's `HH AY1 ER0` root) each build on a stem CMUdict already reads;
# `monticule`'s first syllable is anchored on `monarch`/`montage` (`M AA1
# N...`) rather than `mount` — despite the shared Latin root, English "mount"
# diphthongised while this learned borrowing kept the short vowel, the same
# split as `monument`/`monarch` — and the rest have no CMUdict stem to build
# on and are authored directly from the real pronunciation: `papule`
# (/ˈpæpjuːl/, dermatology), `stipule` (/ˈstɪpjuːl/, botany), `cellule`
# (/ˈsɛljuːl/, "small cell"), `pinnule` (/ˈpɪnjuːl/, biology), `floccule`
# (/ˈflɑːkjuːl/, chemistry), `bascule` (/ˈbæskjuːl/, as in a bascule bridge),
# `fascicule` (/ˈfæsɪkjuːl/, a part-issued volume), `animalcule`
# (/ˌænɪˈmælkjuːl/, the historical "microscopic organism" term), `frustule`
# (/ˈfrʌstjuːl/, a diatom's shell), `pilule` (/ˈpɪljuːl/, "small pill"),
# `gallinule` (/ˈɡælɪnjuːl/, the marsh bird), `crepuscule` (/krɪˈpʌskjuːl/,
# "twilight").
papule P AE1 P Y UW0 L
stipule S T IH1 P Y UW0 L
cellule S EH1 L Y UW0 L
pinnule P IH1 N Y UW0 L
barbule B AA1 R B Y UW0 L
ductule D AH1 K T Y UW0 L
floccule F L AA1 K Y UW0 L
bascule B AE1 S K Y UW0 L
amygdule AH0 M IH1 G D Y UW0 L
plumule P L UW1 M Y UW0 L
noctule N AA1 K T Y UW0 L
monticule M AA1 N T AH0 K Y UW0 L
fascicule F AE1 S AH0 K Y UW0 L
animalcule AE2 N AH0 M AE1 L K Y UW0 L
frustule F R AH1 S T Y UW0 L
pilule P IH1 L Y UW0 L
gemmule JH EH1 M Y UW0 L
gallinule G AE1 L AH0 N Y UW0 L
hierodule HH AY1 ER0 AH0 D Y UW0 L
spherule S F IH1 R UW0 L
crepuscule K R IH0 P AH1 S K Y UW0 L

# spicule: hand-authored, digits copied verbatim from `saccule` (already
# upstream, `S AE1 K Y UW2 L`) — same shape, initial cluster and vowel swapped.
spicule S P IH1 K Y UW2 L

# majuscule: hand-authored, digits copied verbatim from `minuscule` (already
# upstream, `M IH1 N AH0 S K Y UW2 L`) — its own antonym, same shape.
majuscule M AE1 JH AH0 S K Y UW2 L

# ferrule, ferule: hand-authored. Near-homophones (a metal collar; a flat
# rod), each independently attested with two GA pronunciations — one with the
# final vowel reduced to a schwa (`FER-uhl`, not on the target and not
# asserted here), and one with the full `/uːl/` this asserts: `ferrule`
# stress-final (`fe-ROOL`), `ferule` stress-initial with the final syllable
# carrying secondary (`FER-ool`). Both are genuine alternate readings, not
# inventions — this does not say the schwa reading is wrong, only that the
# `/uːl/ one is also real.
ferrule F ER0 UW1 L
ferule F EH1 R UW2 L

# lobule: derived from the upstream inflection `lobules L AA1 B Y UW2 L Z` by
# dropping the final `Z` — rhymes on UW L, with `pool`.
lobule L AA1 B Y UW2 L

# cagoule: hand-authored. French loanword (a hooded rain jacket), real
# `/kəˈɡuːl/`, `kuh-GOOL` — unstressed first syllable, primary stress on the
# final, no promotion needed.
cagoule K AH0 G UW1 L

# pule: hand-authored. A live (if rare) modern dictionary headword — "to cry
# weakly or querulously" — despite looking, at a glance, like it might be a
# prevalence-norm artefact; same monosyllabic shape as `mule`/`yule` already
# upstream.
pule P Y UW1 L

# enschedule: hand-authored, compositional. Archaic ("to insert in a
# schedule; to enroll" — Webster's 1913 Unabridged) but real, not a
# prevalence-norm artefact. `en-` is not one of this repo's configured
# prefixes (affixes.ts), so this is hand-authored rather than derived, by
# direct analogy to how `un-`/`re-`/`mis-` are composed there — an unstressed
# prefix, never primary — prepended to `schedule`'s own second upstream
# reading (`S K EH1 JH UW0 L`) unchanged.
enschedule EH0 N S K EH1 JH UW0 L

